1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,651
Here's a puzzle.

2
00:00:01,701 --> 00:00:06,843
This project's history holds hundreds of files
and half a gigabyte of finished videos.

3
00:00:07,163 --> 00:00:08,850
Now, I create a branch.

4
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:11,908
One command —
and it finishes faster than this sentence.

5
00:00:12,228 --> 00:00:16,132
If a branch really copied the project,
that speed would be impossible.

6
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:17,581
So pause for a second.

7
00:00:17,581 --> 00:00:21,655
What is the smallest thing git could write down,
to remember a branch?

8
00:00:29,067 --> 00:00:33,088
The answer sits in one tiny file
inside the dot git folder:

9
00:00:33,088 --> 00:00:34,417
refs, heads, idea.

10
00:00:34,467 --> 00:00:35,506
Forty-one bytes:

11
00:00:35,506 --> 00:00:38,819
forty characters naming one commit,
plus a newline.

12
00:00:39,139 --> 00:00:44,003
That is the entire branch —
these exact bytes are from a real repository.

13
00:00:44,167 --> 00:00:45,717
Nothing was copied.

14
00:00:45,767 --> 00:00:47,567
Git wrote one line.

15
00:00:53,633 --> 00:00:55,980
The real structure lives in the commits.

16
00:00:56,300 --> 00:01:00,716
Each commit stores a snapshot of your files,
plus the name of its parent commit.

17
00:01:00,866 --> 00:01:03,810
Follow the parents,
and the commits chain into a graph.

18
00:01:04,130 --> 00:01:07,117
That graph already holds
every version of the project.

19
00:01:07,266 --> 00:01:09,066
A branch owns none of this.

20
00:01:09,122 --> 00:01:10,922
It simply points at one node.

21
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:18,900
Watch the pointer do its job.

22
00:01:19,148 --> 00:01:23,159
You are on idea, and a second pointer,
called HEAD, remembers that.

23
00:01:23,333 --> 00:01:27,341
Commit. Git stores the new snapshot,
records its parent —

24
00:01:27,341 --> 00:01:29,801
and then moves exactly one pointer.

25
00:01:29,801 --> 00:01:31,888
Idea now names the new commit.

26
00:01:32,067 --> 00:01:33,553
Main never moved.

27
00:01:33,603 --> 00:01:37,507
The entire difference
between your two branches is two small files.

28
00:01:37,667 --> 00:01:42,659
Think about it: if a branch is just a pointer,
what does being on a branch actually mean?

29
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:53,099
Git switch main moves HEAD back,

30
00:01:53,099 --> 00:01:56,246
and rebuilds your working folder
from main's snapshot.

31
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:58,057
Nothing about idea is lost.

32
00:01:58,057 --> 00:02:02,842
Its forty-one bytes still name its commit,
and the graph keeps every snapshot.

33
00:02:07,633 --> 00:02:11,058
Back on main,
git merge idea creates one new commit —

34
00:02:11,058 --> 00:02:12,426
with two parents.

35
00:02:12,476 --> 00:02:15,889
One line of ancestry comes from main,
one from idea.

36
00:02:16,566 --> 00:02:22,582
This is real: the newest merge in this very
repository lists exactly two parent lines.

37
00:02:22,733 --> 00:02:26,104
The graph grew by one node,
and main moved by one line.

38
00:02:26,424 --> 00:02:28,224
That's all a merge is.

39
00:02:34,167 --> 00:02:40,183
Recap. A branch is a forty-one byte pointer.
Commits and their parents are the real graph.

40
00:02:40,334 --> 00:02:45,262
Committing moves the current branch's pointer.
Merging adds one commit with two parents.

41
00:02:45,434 --> 00:02:48,826
That is why branches are free —
so make one for everything.

42
00:02:49,146 --> 00:02:53,455
Then open dot git, refs, heads,
and read your branch with your own eyes.
